Hi -- the rows correspond to the APE nodes in the tree, you can see their order with the prt(tr) function (prints the APE tree to a table).  The tip nodes are first, then the root node, then internal nodes.

The columns are the states. Each state is a range, a range is made up of zero to many areas. To construct a ranges list see:

http://phylo.wikidot.com/example-biogeobears-scripts#list_of_ranges

For anything except very simple changes in areas, it is better to manually modify the states list (instead of e.g. using the areas_allowed matrix):
http://phylo.wikidot.com/example-biogeobears-scripts#manual_modify_states

Cheers!
Nick

